# Git Workflow Rules

## NEVER Push to `main` Without Explicit Permission

Do NOT run `git push origin main` (or any push to `main`) unless the user has
explicitly said something like:
- "push to main"
- "commit and push to main"
- "deploy to main"

### What You CAN Do Without Asking
- Make code changes locally (edit files)
- Run `git add` and `git commit` locally
- Create local branches
- Run tests locally

### What Requires Explicit Permission
- `git push origin main` — NEVER do this proactively
- `git push` to any remote branch — ask first
- Force pushes — always ask

## Correct Workflow

1. Make the code change locally
2. Tell the user what was changed and why
3. WAIT for the user to say "push it" or "commit and push to main"
4. Only then run `git add && git commit && git push origin main`

## If You Made an Unauthorized Push

Immediately revert using `git revert` (not `git reset --hard`) to preserve history,
then push the revert commit.
